<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">He Chaozong</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. early 17th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">He Chaozong was a Chinese potter of the early 17th century who made mainly Buddhist statues in white <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porcelain">porcelain</a> in the tradition of the Dehua kilns in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujian">Fujian</a> Province, known in the West as Blanc-de-Chine. Little documentary evidence of him survives beyond his works, though a gazetteer entry of 1763 names him among the art notables of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming dynasty</a>. The figures identified by his seal are thickly potted and more expressive than the thinner, standardised unsigned works of the kilns, and he was one of the few named artists in a ceramic history otherwise marked by anonymous, industrial production. A revival of his manner in the late 19th and early 20th centuries produced works bearing his seal.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/he-chaozong.jpg" alt="He Chaozong" width="330" height="495" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Iwanafish · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:He_Chaozong_1.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
